In article <ARI.HUTTUNEN.91Apr16010133@wonderwoman.hut.fi> Ari.Huttunen@hut.fi (Ari Juhani Huttunen) writes:
>In article <1991Apr15.104915.8707@gdr.bath.ac.uk> exspes@gdr.bath.ac.uk (P E Smee) writes:
>>there should be some other mechanism added to the game to ensure that
>>required items would be generated somewhere.
>
>How do you define 'required items'? Right now I can't think of an item,
>that you can't win without.

Well, maybe 'required classes', then.  E.g. you do need one of the ways of
teleporting, one of the ways of controlling it, and a way of becoming
confused.  There seems to be general agreement that, practically speaking,
you need a blindfold before you come to the Medusa level.  You need some
way of making yourself fireproof.  Come to that, you need some way out
of each room, to take my original example.  Probably other things that
I've missed.  

Some are tricky (it just occurs to me), like you need enough food to
allow you to get in and back out, but at the same time, you don't want
to provide so much that people can screw around polypiling (or troll
dancing, or whatever) for unlimited periods of time.  Indeed, some of
the problems might in principle be unsolvable (there's a challenge for
the dev team :-).  Your idea of some sort of guaranteed minimums for
various items or classes might work for a lot of them.  E.g. if no
blindfold has been generated by level X, then level X will have one.
If the odds governing generation are reasonable, then there should only
be a small chance if the clause being applied anyway.


